<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Clement VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1478">1478</a>–<a href="/years/1534">1534</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Giulio de' Medici</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Clement VII (1478–1534), born Giulio de' Medici, was pope from 1523 to 1534. The illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuliano-de-medici">Giuliano de' Medici</a>, he governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> for his cousin Pope Leo X before his election. His shifting alliances between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527, after which he made peace with Charles and used imperial troops to restore the Medici to Florence in 1530.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii.jpg" alt="Pope Clement VII" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastiano del Piombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_papa_Clemente_VII,_por_Sebastiano_del_Piombo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
